3	== Specification/appendices/changes.tex:1297-1396
4	
5	\subsection{The traits that extend a closed trait}
6	\seclabel{revival-comprises}
7	
8	\begin{description}
9	\item[Affected sections] \secref{trait-decls}.
10	\item[Change] The first sentence of a draft note in \secref{trait-decls},
11	which said that the traits listed in a \KWD{comprises} clause are exactly
12	the traits that immediately extend the declared trait and that they must
13	explicitly extend it, is replaced by normative text: every listed type
14	extends the declared trait \VAR{T}, and every value of type \VAR{T} is a
15	value of one of the listed types; a listed type may be an instantiation of
16	a parameterized trait whose static arguments contain no static variable
17	other than the static parameters of \VAR{T}; and a trait or object that explicitly extends
18	\VAR{T} is a subtype of a listed type, or a trait with a \KWD{comprises}
19	clause of its own each of whose listed types meets the same requirement, or
20	a trait with static parameters that at least one trait or object extends
21	and every trait or object extending it is a subtype of a listed type.
22	The note's second sentence, on a clause that includes ``\EXP{\ldots}'', is
23	kept in its note, and the example of \TYP{Molecule} is unchanged.
24	The new text answers the third question of Victor Luchangco's draft note
25	on trait identifiers earlier in the section, whether an instantiation of a
26	parameterized trait may be listed: it may.
27	\item[Rationale] This change does not follow from route~A; the revival
28	decided it on 28~September 2026.
29	The team's later Types chapter (Luchangco, 2012) reads a \KWD{comprises}
30	clause as coverage: the clause ``specifies a set of types and generic types
31	determined by the generic type of the declaration'', and an instantiation of
32	the declared generic type ``is covered by the union of the types in this set
33	and the corresponding instantiations of the generic type in this set'', one
34	generic type $G$ determining another $G'$ ``if every parameter of $G'$ is a
35	parameter of $G$''
36	(\nolinkurl{Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/types.tick}, lines
37	277--283 and 384--390, in the revival's repository).
38	Welterweight Fortress, the calculus of the type group's paper of 2012
39	(Chase, Hilburn, Luchangco, Naden, Ryu, Steele, and Tristan, ``Dynamic
40	Dispatch and Type Inference Semipredicates''), reads it at the level of
41	values: ``no value can belong to the trait unless it also belongs to one of
42	the comprised types''; its rule for a trait declaration asks nothing of the
43	traits that extend a trait with such a clause
44	(\nolinkurl{Papers/Welterweight/grammar.tick}, lines 21--22;
45	\nolinkurl{Papers/Welterweight/fig-wellformeddecls.tick}, lines 38--62).
46	The draft note restated the rule of the compiled type checker of 2009,
47	under which \library\ could not close their marker of the integer types as
48	the team meant to: \TYP{AnyIntegral} lists the five integer types, and the
49	parameterized trait \EXP{\TYP{Integral}\llbracket{}I\rrbracket}, which each
50	of them extends at itself, stands between them unlisted, as the team's
51	comment on the declaration records (``not yet: comprises
52	\EXP{\TYP{Integral}\llbracket{}I\rrbracket} where
53	\EXP{\llbracket{}I\rrbracket}'', \nolinkurl{Library/FortressLibrary.fsi},
54	line 434).
55	\TYP{Integral} has a static parameter that \TYP{AnyIntegral} does not have,
56	so it can be listed only at particular static arguments, and every value of it is a value of one of the five.
57	The full reasoning is in
58	\nolinkurl{explorations/compile-ladder/rung-spec-comprises/decision-record.md}
59	in the revival's repository.
60	\item[Effect] The compiled type checker accepts the declaration of
61	\TYP{AnyIntegral} in \library\ as written
62	(\nolinkurl{Library/FortressLibrary.fsi}, lines 433--436).
63	No example of this specification changes: \TYP{ExclusiveMolecule}, which
64	has no static parameters, is still not allowed.
65	The compiled type checker enforces the requirement on a trait with static
66	parameters over the declarations and object expressions it sees: a trait or object declared in
67	another component, below such a trait and a subtype of no listed type, is
68	not refused (row~487 of the revival's gap ledger). It compares the declarations it sees by their names: for a parameterized \VAR{T} its verdict can depend on how an extender spells its static parameters, and a trait or object that extends a trait of the same simple name in another API counts as an extender (rows~489 and~490).
69	It refuses an object expression that extends a trait with a \KWD{comprises}
70	clause, explicitly or through the traits it extends, and is a subtype of no
71	listed type, as the requirement on the trait's extenders together demands of
72	it, an object expression having neither static parameters nor a clause of its
73	own; before 3~October 2026 it read no object expression against a clause
74	(row~597).
75	The interpreter checks at load the traits and objects that explicitly extend a
76	trait with a \KWD{comprises} clause declared in the component it executes, and
77	refuses one that is neither a subtype of a listed type nor another of the
78	extenders that \secref{trait-decls} allows.
79	It does not yet check a clause declared in another component, of the program
80	or of \library\ (rows~22, 487, 551, 595 and~596), an object expression that
81	extends such a trait (row~597), or that each listed type extends \VAR{T}
82	(row~598).
83	\item[Original text] \nolinkurl{Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/traits.tex},
84	lines 230--241, a draft note:
85	\begin{quote}
86	If a trait declaration of \VAR{T} includes a \KWD{comprises} clause
87	then the traits listed in its \KWD{comprises} clause
88	are exactly the traits that immediately extend \VAR{T}
89	and they must explicitly extend \VAR{T}
90	(i.e., list \VAR{T} in their \KWD{extends} clause).
91	If the \KWD{comprises} clause of a trait \VAR{T} includes ``\EXP{\ldots}''
92	(and must therefore be in an API),
93	then in a component exporting the enclosing API,
94	other traits may explicitly extend \VAR{T},
95	but these traits may not be declared or imported by the API
96	(see \secref{apis}).
97	\end{quote}
98	Its second sentence is kept, in its note.
99	\item[Route C] The same under route~C, under which the self-typed traits
100	carry \KWD{comprises} clauses of their own (\secref{revival-routec}): a
101	parameterized trait between a closed trait and its listed types then meets
102	the requirement through its own clause, and the last case of the
103	requirement is not needed.
104	\end{description}
